NNAAMMEE
       pamsumm - Summarize the samples in a Netpbm image arithmetically


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppaammssuummmm  {  --ssuumm  | --mmeeaann | --mmiinn | --mmaaxx } [--nnoorrmmaalliizzee] [--bbrriieeff] [_i_m_a_g_e_‐
       _f_i_l_e]

       All options can be abbreviated to their shortest  unique  prefix.   You
       may  use  two  hyphens instead of one.  You may separate an option name
       and its value with white space instead of an equals sign.


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppaammssuummmm reads a Netpbm image (PNM or PAM) and performs a summary  func‐
       tion  over  all  the  samples  in all the rows, columns, and planes and
       prints the result to Standard Output.

       ppaammssuummmm performs the operation on the actual  sample  values.   In  the
       case  of  a PGM or PPM or PAM equivalent (i.e. a visual image), this is
       not the same as the light intensities  represented  by  those  samples.
       See  the  format  specifications of PPGGMM(1), PPPPMM(1), and PPAAMM(1), for the
       precise meanings of samples in these formats.  If you want to do arith‐
       metic on light intensities of such a visual image, you can use ppnnmmggaammmmaa
       to convert it to one with samples proportional to light intensity,  and
       then use ppaammssuummmm on the result.

       If  you  want  to  summarize  by  column (e.g. add up the columns sepa‐
       rately), use ppaammssuummmmccooll.  If you want to summarize by row, use a combi‐
       nation  of ppaammssuummmmccooll and ppaammfflliipp.  If you want to summarize a particu‐
       lar plane, use ppaammcchhaannnneell to extract it and then ppaammssuummmm.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩  ), ppaammssuummmm recognizes the
       following command line options:

       You must specify exactly one of --ssuumm, --mmeeaann, --mmiinn, or --mmaaxx.



       --ssuumm

              This option makes the summary function addition.


       --mmeeaann

              This option makes the summary function arithmetic mean.


       --mmiinn

              This option makes the summary function arithmetic minimum.


       --mmaaxx

              This option makes the summary function arithmetic maximum.


       --nnoorrmmaalliizzee

              This option causes each sample to be normalized to a fraction
                   (in the range 0..1) so the result is independent of the im‐
              age's
                   maxval.  E.g. if you request the mean of an image which has
              maxval
                   200 and all the samples have value 50,  ppaammssuummmm  will  give
              you
                   50 as an answer.  But ppaammssuummmm --nnoorrmmaalliizzee will give you .25.

              If  instead  you want a result that is independent of maxval but
              still
                   in integers, you can use ppaammddeepptthh to convert the  input  to
              some
                   standard  maxval  and  not use --nnoorrmmaalliizzee.  For example, if
              you want
                   the mean brightness of a PPM image, on a scale of 0 to  99,
              do

                  pamdepth 99 myimage.ppm | pamsumm -mean


              This option was new in Netpbm 10.22 (April 2004)


       --bbrriieeff

              This option causes ppaammssuummmm to display the answer as a bare
                   number, rather than in a complete sentence.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.22 (April 2004)




